thout complications, as part of the treatment for the patient's health issues. However, it was also noted that liver transplantation was evaluated as a treatment option for the patient with MNGIE (Mitochondrial Neurogastrointestinal Encephalomyopathy) but was ultimately deemed unsuitable. This indicates that while the transplantation was successfully carried out in 2003, it was not considered an appropriate option for the specific condition of MNGIE.MERCEDES V√ÅZQUEZ GUTI√âRREZ:Dra. Mercedes V√°zquez Guti√©rrez is a specialist in Endocrinology and Nutrition at the Hospital Universitario de Torrec√°rdenas in Almer√≠a.HOSPITAL UNIVERSITARIO DE TORREC√ÅRDENAS:Hospital Universitario de Torrec√°rdenas is a clinical unit specializing in Endocrinology and Nutrition located in Almer√≠a.PANCREATITIS AGUDA:Acute pancreatitis is a medical condition characterized by inflammation of the pancreas, which can lead to tissue damage and affect other organs. This condition often manifests with symptoms that arise between 24 and 36 hours following the peak production of cytokines. In the case of the patient, acute pancreatitis was experienced prior to the diagnosis of a tumor, necessitating intensive treatment and monitoring. Additionally, careful nutritional management is crucial for patients with acute pancreatitis, particularly for those who are diabetic.UCI:The Unidad de Cuidados Intensivos (UCI), also known as the Intensive Care Unit, is a specialized area within a hospital dedicated to providing critical care to patients facing severe health challenges. This unit is particularly equipped to manage patients with serious conditions, such as severe pancreatitis, and is designed to handle complications that may arise during treatment. Patients requiring intensive monitoring and advanced medical interventions are transferred to the UCI to ensure they receive the highest level of care during critical situations.DUODENOPANCREATECTOM√çA SUBTOTAL:Subtotal duodenopancreatectomy is the surgical procedure performed on the patient to address the pancreatic tumor.TAC:TAC, or Tomograf√≠a Axial Computarizada, is a medical imaging technique utilized to monitor a patient's condition over time, particularly for the detection of lesions. In this case, a CT scan was performed, revealing irregularities in the pancreatic process along with other findings. The procedure specifically aimed to confirm the dilation of the Wirsung duct in the patient, highlighting its importance in diagnosing and assessing pancreatic conditions.RMN:Magnetic Resonance Imaging (RMN) was conducted to suggest the presence of a mucinous tumor in the patient.BIOPSIA:A biopsy was performed via endoscopic ultrasound to confirm the diagnosis of the pancreatic mucinous tumor.HEMODI√ÅLISIS:Hemodialysis was required by the patient due to renal failure during their stay in the UCI.SANGRADO MASIVO:Massive bleeding occurred post-surgery, necessitating urgent intervention for the patient.DEHISCENCIA DE LA ANASTOMOSIS PANCREATOYEYUNAL:Dehiscence of the pancreatojejunostomy occurred, leading to further complications for the patient.PANCREATITIS AGUDA NECR√ìTICO HEMORR√ÅGICA:Acute necrotizing hemorrhagic pancreatitis developed in the patient, complicating their recovery.COMPLICACIONES:COMPLICACIONES refer to the various health issues that arise as a result of a patient's diabetes. During hospitalization, the patient experienced multiple complications related to their condition and treatment, highlighting the significant impact that diabetes can have on overall health and the potential challenges faced in managing the disease.VAR√ìN:F√çSTULA PANCRE√ÅTICA:La f√≠stula pancre√°tica es una complicaci√≥n m√©dica que se presenta como una conexi√≥n anormal entre el p√°ncreas y otros √≥rganos, en este caso, el paciente presenta una f√≠stula externa de d√©bito bajo-moderado.ENZIMAS PANCRE√ÅTICAS:ENZIMAS PANCRE√ÅTICAS son suplementos prescritos a pacientes que sufren de insuficiencia pancre√°tica, con el objetivo de ayudar en la digesti√≥n y mejorar la absorci√≥n de nutrientes. Estas enzimas son especialmente administradas a pacientes con fibrosis qu√≠stica, ya que su uso es crucial para facilitar la digesti√≥n y prevenir la desnutrici√≥n en estos individuos.VITAMINA D:VITAMINA D es un suplemento que se ha incorporado al tratamiento de los pacientes debido a su d√©ficit. Se prescribe con el objetivo de mejorar el estado nutricional y la salud √≥sea de los pacientes. Adem√°s, la suplementaci√≥n con vitamina D es recomendada para ayudar a mantener la calidad de vida y la salud general.VITAMINA B12:VITAMINA B12 is a vital nutrient that plays a significant role in various bodily functions, including nerve function, DNA production, and the formation of red blood cells. It is often prescribed as a supplement to patients to prevent nutritional deficiencies. Additionally, it is particularly important to monitor vitamin B12 levels in patients with cystic fibrosis, as they may experience absorption issues that can lead to deficiencies. Regular blood tests are conducted to measure vitamin B12 levels, ensuring that patients maintain adequate amounts of this essential vitamin for their overall health.EQUIPO M√âDICO:El equipo m√©dico est√° compuesto por profesionales de la salud que supervisan y tratan al paciente durante su recuperaci√≥n.S√çNTOMAS CL√çNICOS:Los s√≠ntomas cl√≠nicos incluyen hiperglucemia, esteatorrea y p√©rdida de peso, que el paciente ha estado experimentando durante su tratamiento.DIFICULTAD PARA AUMENTAR PESO:El paciente ha reportado dificultad para aumentar de peso a pesar de seguir las indicaciones diet√©ticas y terap√©uticas.F√ìRMULA POLIM√âRICA NORMOCAL√ìRICA:La f√≥rmula polim√©rica normocal√≥rica es un suplemento nutricional que se prescribe al paciente para ayudar a cubrir sus requerimientos energ√©ticos.F√ìRMULA HIPERCAL√ìRICA ESPEC√çFICA DE DIABETES:La f√≥rmula hipercal√≥rica espec√≠fica de diabetes es un tipo de suplemento que se utiliza para ayudar al paciente a aumentar de peso mientras controla su glucemia.DESNUTRICI√ìN SEVERA:ENZYMES PANCRE√ÅTICAS:Enzymes pancre√°ticas are supplements used to aid digestion in patients with pancreatic insufficiency, particularly in those with diabetes.F√ìRMULAS NUTRICIONALES ESPEC√çFICAS:F√≥rmulas nutricionales espec√≠ficas are specialized dietary formulas designed to meet the nutritional needs of diabetic patients.CIRUG√çA DIGESTIVA:CIRUG√çA DIGESTIVA refers to surgical procedures involving the digestive tract, which require careful nutritional management postoperatively.MULTIDISCIPLINAR:Multidisciplinar refers to the collaborative approach involving various healthcare professionals to manage complex medical conditions like diabetes.HORMONAS REGULADORAS:Hormonas reguladoras are hormones that regulate carbohydrate metabolism, including insulin and glucagon, which are affected by pancreatic surgery.NUTRIENTES:Nutrientes are essential substances obtained from food that are necessary for the body's growth, metabolism, and overall health, particularly important in diabetes management.PANCREATIC RESECTION:SPANISH SOCIETY OF ENDOCRINOLOGY AND NUTRITION:The Spanish Society of Endocrinology and Nutrition (SEEN) is an organization focused on the study and management of endocrine and nutritional issues, including diabetes and metabolic control.A.S.P.E.N.:The American Society for Parenteral and Enteral Nutrition (A.S.P.E.N.) provides clinical guidelines for nutrition support in patients, particularly those with hyperglycemia.GARIN GROUP:The GARIN group is the Andalusian Group for Nutrition Reflection and Investigation, which focuses on managing patients with diabetes and stress hyperglycemia.HYPERGLYCEMIA:Hyperglycemia is a medical condition characterized by an excessive amount of glucose in the blood. It is often seen in patients with diabetes and may require dietary intervention to manage blood sugar levels effectively. If left untreated, hyperglycemia can lead to severe complications, highlighting the importance of monitoring and controlling blood sugar levels in affected individuals.PANCREATICODUODENECTOMY:Pancreaticoduodenectomy is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of the pancreas and duodenum, often requiring specialized nutritional support post-surgery.NUTRITION SUPPORT:Nutrition support refers to the provision of nutrients and nutritional therapy to patients who are unable to eat or absorb sufficient nutrients due to various medical conditions, such as acute pancreatitis. This support is essential for individuals who cannot meet their nutritional needs through normal eating, ensuring they receive the necessary nutrients for recovery and overall health.TAPIA MJ:Tapia MJ is a researcher who co-authored studies on the prevalence of diabetes and its management in parenteral nutrition.OC√ìN J:Oc√≥n J is a contributor to research on insulin therapy and metabolic control in patients on total parenteral nutrition.CABREJAS-G√ìMEZ C:Cabrejas-G√≥mez C is part of the study group focusing on hyperglycemia in parenteral nutrition.BALLENTEROS-POMAR MD:Ballenteros-Pomar MD is a researcher involved in the study of diabetes management in parenteral nutrition.GONZALEZ INFANTINO CA:Gonzalez Infantino CA is an author who studied hyperglycemia and hypoalbuminemia as mortality factors in patients with enteral feeding.GONZ√ÅLEZ CD:Gonz√°lez CD is a researcher who contributed to studies on hyperglycemia in patients receiving enteral nutrition.SANCHEZ R:Sanchez R is an author involved in research on hyperglycemia and its effects on patients with enteral feeding.PRESNER N:Presner N is a researcher who studied the impact of hyperglycemia in enteral feeding patients.LASSEN K:Lassen K is a researcher who contributed to guidelines for perioperative care in pancreaticoduodenectomy.COOLSEN MM:Coolsen MM is involved in research comparing feeding regimes after pancreaticoduodenectomy.SLIM K:Slim K is a researcher who contributed to guidelines for the care of patients undergoing pancreaticoduodenectomy.CARLI F:Carli F is a researcher involved in the study of perioperative care for pancreaticoduodenectomy patients.DE AGUILAR- NACIMIENTO JE:De Aguilar-Nacimiento JE is a contributor to research on postoperative care in pancreaticoduodenectomy.SCH√ÑFER M:Sch√§fer M is a researcher who contributed to guidelines for managing patients post-pancreaticoduodenectomy.GUILBAUD T:Guilbaud T is a researcher who compared different feeding regimes after pancreaticoduodenectomy.BIRNBAUM DJ:Birnbaum DJ is involved in research on feeding regimes after pancreaticoduodenectomy.LOUBI√àRE S:Loub√®re S is a researcher who contributed to studies on feeding regimes post-surgery.BONNET J:Bonnet J is a researcher involved in the analysis of feeding regimes after pancreaticoduodenectomy.CHOPINET S:Chopinet S is a researcher who contributed to studies on nutritional support after pancreaticoduodenectomy.GR√âGOIRE E:Gr√©goire E is involved in research comparing feeding regimes after pancreaticoduodenectomy.BERDAH S:Berdah S is a researcher who contributed to studies on nutritional support in surgical patients.HARDWIGSEN J:Hardwigsen J is involved in research on postoperative care and nutrition.MOUTARDIER V:Moutardier V is a researcher who contributed to studies on feeding regimes after pancreaticoduodenectomy.DAS BC:Das BC is a researcher who studied the effects of enteral feeding timing in post-pancreaticoduodenectomy patients.HAQUE M:Haque M is involved in research on enteral feeding in patients after pancreaticoduodenectomy.UDDIN MS:Uddin MS is a researcher who studied the effects of enteral feeding in post-surgical patients.NUR-E-ELAHI M:Nur-E-Elahi M is involved in research on enteral feeding in patients with diabetes.KHAN ZR:Khan ZR is a researcher who studied the effects of enteral feeding in diabetic patients.MC MAHON MM:McMahon MM is a contributor to clinical guidelines for nutrition support in hyperglycemic patients.NYSTROM E:Nystrom E is involved in the development of clinical guidelines for nutrition support.BRAUNSCHWEIG C:Braunschweig C is a researcher who contributed to clinical guidelines for managing hyperglycemia.MILES J:Miles J is involved in the development of clinical guidelines for nutrition support in patients with diabetes.COMPHER C:Compher C is a contributor to clinical guidelines for nutrition support in hyperglycemic patients.ALEJANDRO SANZ-PAR√çS:Alejandro Sanz-Par√≠s is a researcher involved in studies on enteral nutrition formulas for diabetes management.DIANA BOJ-CARCELLER:Diana Boj-Carceller is a researcher who contributed to studies on enteral nutrition for diabetic patients.BEATRIZ LARDIES-SANCHEZ:Beatriz Lardies-Sanchez is involved in research on enteral nutrition for diabetes management.LETICIA PEREZ-FERN√ÅNDEZ:Leticia Perez-Fern√°ndez is a researcher who contributed to studies on nutrition for diabetic patients.)<|COMPLETE|>DANIEL A. DE LUIS ROM√ÅN:Dr. Daniel A. de Luis Rom√°n is the scientific director and head of the Endocrinology and Nutrition Service at Hospital Cl√≠nico Universitario de Valladolid.UNIVERSIDAD DE VALLADOLID:Universidad de Valladolid is the university where Prof. Dr. Daniel de Luis Rom√°n is a professor of Endocrinology and Nutrition.ANA ISABEL S√ÅNCHEZ MARCOS:Dra. Ana Isabel S√°nchez Marcos is a specialist in Endocrinology and Nutrition, focusing on eating disorders in patients with diabetes.TRASTORNO DEL COMPORTAMIENTO ALIMENTARIO:"TRASTORNO DEL COMPORTAMIENTO ALIMENTARIO," or Eating Disorders (TCA), refers to a group of psychiatric diseases that are marked by significant medical complications. These disorders are characterized by persistent disturbances in food intake, which can impair both health and psychosocial functioning. Additionally, this condition notably affects patients with diabetes, particularly young women diagnosed with Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus (DM1).DIABETES MELLITUS TIPO 1:Diabetes Mellitus Tipo 1 (DM1) is a chronic condition that has been managed by the patient for 6 years, characterized by poor glycemic control. This condition occurs when the pancreas produces little or no insulin, necessitating continuous management of insulin and blood glucose levels. Additionally, Diabetes Mellitus Tipo 1 poses a higher risk for developing eating disorders, particularly in young women, which can complicate the careful management required for the condition. Overall, DM1 demands vigilant monitoring and a comprehensive approach to treatment to maintain health and mitigate associated risks.HYPERCALORIC DIABETES-SPECIFIC ENTERAL NUTRITIONAL FORMULA:A specialized nutritional formula designed for patients with diabetes to improve their nutritional status and glycemic control.DIABETES TECHNOLOGY:Diabetes technology refers to the tools and methods used to manage diabetes, including enteral formulas and monitoring devices.DIABETES MELLITUS TYPE 1:Diabetes Mellitus Type 1 (DM1) is an autoimmune disease characterized by chronic insulin deficiency. This condition necessitates careful management of diet and physical activity to effectively control blood sugar levels. It is a chronic condition that can lead to various complications, and in this particular case, it was diagnosed in the patient at the age of 28.AMERICAN PSYCHIATRIC ASSOCIATION:The American Psychiatric Association is a professional organization that publishes the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM-5), which includes criteria for diagnosing eating disorders.FUNDACI√ìN SED:The Fundaci√≥n SED is an organization in Spain that provides data and support for individuals with diabetes, including statistics on the prevalence of diabetes in minors.ANOREXIA NERVIOSA:Anorexia Nervosa is an eating disorder marked by an intense fear of gaining weight and a distorted body image, leading to severe food restriction and health risks.TRASTORNO DE EVITACI√ìN/RESTRICCI√ìN DE ALIMENTOS:Avoidant/Restrictive Food Intake Disorder is characterized by a lack of interest in eating or avoidance of certain foods, which can lead to nutritional deficiencies.TRASTORNO DE ATRACONES:Binge Eating Disorder involves recurrent episodes of eating large quantities of food, often to the point of discomfort, and is associated with feelings of loss of control.PICA:Pica is an eating disorder characterized by the persistent consumption of non-nutritive, non-food substances, which can lead to serious health complications.TRASTORNO DE RUMIACI√ìN:Rumination Disorder involves the repeated regurgitation of food, which may be re-chewed, re-swallowed, or spit out, and can have significant health implications.CIE-9:The International Classification of Diseases, 9th Revision (ICD-9) is a system used for coding and classifying diseases, including eating disorders.CIE-10:The International Classification of Diseases, 10th Revision (ICD-10) is an updated system for coding diseases, providing guidelines for the classification of eating disorders.BULIMIA NERVIOSA:MUJER DM1:A female patient diagnosed with Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus (DM1) who has experienced poor metabolic control and has a history of eating disorders.ENDOCRINOLOG√çA PEDI√ÅTRICA:Pediatric endocrinology department that provided follow-up care for the patient transitioning to adult care.MADRE:The mother of the patient who has a history of thyroid crises and is a smoker, influencing the patient's health.ABUELA:The grandmother of the patient who has a diagnosis of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus (DM2), indicating a family history of diabetes.TCA:TCA, or Trastorno de la Conducta Alimentaria, refers to an eating disorder that the patient is struggling with, characterized by restrictive eating and purging behaviors. This disorder is particularly prevalent among patients with Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus (DM1), especially young women, and it can exacerbate the complications associated with diabetes. The specific manifestations of TCA in this context include food restriction and the omission of insulin, which can significantly impact the patient's health and well-being.CRISIS TIROT√ìXICAS:Thyroid crises experienced by the patient's mother, which may have health implications for the patient.MALA ADAPTACI√ìN SOCIAL ESCOLAR:The poor social adaptation of the patient in school, which may relate to her health and psychological state.INFUSORA SUBCUT√ÅNEA CONTINUA DE INSULINA:The continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion device used by the patient to manage her diabetes.PADRES:UTCA:UTCA, or Unidad de Trastornos de la Conducta Alimentaria, is a specialized healthcare facility dedicated to the treatment of eating disorders. It offers comprehensive care that addresses both nutritional and psychological issues faced by patients. The unit operates through a collaborative approach, integrating the expertise of various disciplines, including Psychiatry, Psychology, and Endocrinology, to provide holistic treatment tailored to the needs of individuals struggling with these disorders.ANAMNESIS:Anamnesis is a detailed assessment process that includes the patient's medical history, dietary habits, and psychological factors related to eating disorders.VALORACI√ìN NUTRICIONAL:The "VALORACI√ìN NUTRICIONAL" refers to the process of assessing a patient's nutritional status and needs. This comprehensive evaluation includes the use of clinical and anthropometric markers to determine the individual's nutritional state. At UTCA, the nutritional assessment encompasses various tests and evaluations aimed at understanding the patient's eating habits and overall nutritional status. Through these methods, "VALORACI√ìN NUTRICIONAL" provides a thorough understanding of a patient's dietary requirements and health.ANTROPOMETR√çA:ANTROPOMETR√çA refers to the practice of measuring the human body's physical dimensions and body composition. This field is essential for assessing an individual's health and nutritional status. By evaluating these measurements, healthcare professionals can gain insights into a person's physical health and overall well-being.ECG:Electrocardiogram (ECG) is a test performed to monitor the patient's heart activity and detect any abnormalities.ANAL√çTICA:ANAL√çTICA refers to the laboratory tests conducted to evaluate a patient's biochemical markers and overall health status. These analytical tests are essential for monitoring various blood parameters, including blood counts, enzyme levels, blood sugar levels, and nutritional deficiencies. By assessing these factors, ANAL√çTICA provides valuable insights into the patient's condition, helping healthcare professionals make informed decisions regarding diagnosis and treatment.TENSION ARTERIAL:Blood pressure measurement is taken to assess the cardiovascular health of the patient.FRECUENCIA CARDIACA:Heart rate measurement is conducted to monitor the patient's heart function and overall health.BIA:Bioelectrical Impedance Analysis (BIA) is a method used to estimate body composition, including fat and muscle mass.DEXA:Dual-Energy X-ray Absorptiometry (DEXA) is a technique used to measure bone density and body composition.DMO:Bone Mineral Density (DMO) assessment is performed to evaluate the strength and health of the patient's bones.HBA1C:HbA1c is a blood test that measures average blood glucose levels over the past 2 to 3 months, making it an essential tool for managing diabetes. It indicates the patient's glycemic control, with values consistently above 8.5% suggesting poor management of blood sugar levels. Throughout treatment, HbA1c levels are monitored to assess the effectiveness of diabetes management strategies. In a recent analysis, the patient's HbA1c result was recorded at 6.2%, indicating good control of her diabetes.HOSPITAL DE D√çA:Hospital de D√≠a is a day hospital where the patient was admitted for treatment from May 2015 to July 2015.FLUOXETINA:Fluoxetine is a medication prescribed to the patient as part of their treatment plan.LORACEPAM:Lorazepam is a medication prescribed to the patient to help manage anxiety as part of their treatment plan.DIAGN√ìSTICO NUTRICIONAL:The nutritional diagnosis indicates the patient's condition, including normonutrition with a decrease in prealbumin and deficiencies in zinc and vitamin D3.TRATAMIENTO INICIAL:The initial treatment proposed for the patient includes dietary recommendations, insulin adjustments, and supplementation for deficiencies.RECUPERACI√ìN NUTRICIONAL:Nutritional recovery involves achieving metabolic stability and addressing the patient's malnutrition.RECUPERACI√ìN PSIQUI√ÅTRICA:Psychiatric recovery focuses on stabilizing the patient's eating behaviors and addressing their restrictive conduct.ZINC:Zinc is a crucial micronutrient that plays a significant role in various bodily functions. It is particularly important for patients with cystic fibrosis, as monitoring zinc levels in these individuals helps prevent deficiencies. Deficiency in zinc can lead to various health issues, making it essential for maintaining overall health and well-being.VITAMINA D3:Vitamin D3 is a vitamin that the patient is deficient in, crucial for bone health and immune function.CETONURIA:Ketonuria is a condition where ketones are present in the urine, indicating poor control of diabetes in the patient.REVISI√ìN TRIMESTRAL:The quarterly review is a follow-up appointment to assess the patient's progress and health status.DM1:DM1 refers to Type 1 Diabetes, which is a chronic condition that the patient is managing in conjunction with an eating disorder.HOSPITAL DE D√çA UTCA:Hospital where the patient was admitted for psychiatric recovery and stabilization of eating behaviors from May 2015 to July 2015VENLAFAXINA:Venlafaxine is an antidepressant medication prescribed to the patient for managing her psychiatric condition, specifically for treating depressive disorders.MONITORIZACI√ìN FLASH DE GLUCOSA:A glucose monitoring system that improved the patient's glycemic controlJULIO 2015:The month when the patient was discharged from Hospital de D√≠a UTCA after stabilizationAGOSTO 2016:The month when the patient was discharged again from Hospital de D√≠a UTCA after a new admissionINSULINA GLULISINA:Type of insulin administered to the patient to manage her diabetesMULTIDOSIS:A method of insulin administration that the patient switched to after her initial treatmentDEGLUDEC:Another type of insulin that the patient was prescribed as part of her diabetes managementMAYO 2015:EQUIPO MULTIDISCIPLINAR:A multidisciplinary team consisting of a psychiatrist, psychologist, endocrinologist, diabetes educator, dietitian/nutritionist, and social worker, working together to manage treatment priorities for patients with diabetes and eating disorders.M. DELGADO G√ìMEZ:Dr. M. Delgado G√≥mez is one of the doctors acknowledged for sharing clinical follow-up of patients with diabetes and eating disorders.D. GONZ√ÅLEZ PARRA:Dr. D. Gonz√°lez Parra is another doctor acknowledged for sharing clinical follow-up of patients with diabetes and eating disorders.L. BARRERA AGUADO:Dr. L. Barrera Aguado is also acknowledged for sharing clinical follow-up of patients with diabetes and eating disorders.AUTOCUIDADO:Self-care refers to the practices that patients with Type 1 diabetes must adopt to manage their condition effectively, including monitoring blood glucose and administering insulin.HIPOGLUCEMIAS:Hypoglycemia is a condition that can occur in patients with diabetes when blood sugar levels drop too low, often requiring immediate intervention.CETOACIDOSIS:Diabetic ketoacidosis is a serious complication of diabetes that can occur when insulin levels are insufficient, leading to high blood sugar and ketone production.ELECTROLITOS:Electrolytes are minerals in the body that must be stabilized during diabetes treatment, especially in cases of vomiting or laxative use.ESTEFAN√çA SANTOS MAZO:Estefan√≠a Santos Mazo is a medical specialist in endocrinology and nutrition at the Hospital Universitario de Burgos.APACHE SCORE:The APACHE score is a severity scoring system used to measure the severity of disease in patients, particularly in critical care settings.NUTRICI√ìN ENTERAL (NE):Nutritional support provided through the gastrointestinal tract, which is essential for patients with severe acute pancreatitis.NUTRICI√ìN PARENTERAL (NP):Nutritional support provided intravenously, used when enteral nutrition is not feasible or sufficient for patients.RANSON CRITERIA:CIRUG√çA GENERAL:CIRUG√çA GENERAL is the medical department responsible for performing surgical procedures, specifically focusing on the evaluation and management of patients with suspected pancreatitis. In this context, the department is involved in the placement of enteral nutrition tubes, ensuring that patients receive the necessary nutritional support during their treatment.PANCREATITIS AGUDA EDEMATOSA:Pancreatitis aguda edematosa is the medical condition diagnosed in the patient, characterized by inflammation of the pancreas.FARMACIA:Farmacia is the pharmacy department responsible for providing medications and nutritional support to the patient.ENDOCRINOLOG√çA Y NUTRICI√ìN:The Endocrinolog√≠a y Nutrici√≥n department is a specialized medical unit that focuses on the evaluation and management of endocrine disorders and nutritional needs. The patient was referred to this department after experiencing significant weight loss, indicating a potential underlying health issue. This department is also consulted for managing the patient's diabetes, highlighting its role in addressing both endocrine and nutritional concerns.VAR√ìN DE 56 A√ëOS:A 56-year-old male patient presenting with abdominal pain and a history of hypertension and type 2 diabetes.HEMANGIOMA CEREBELOSO:Hemangioma cerebeloso is a previous medical condition noted in the patient's history.TC ABDOMINAL:TC abdominal is the imaging study conducted to evaluate the patient's abdominal organs and diagnose pancreatitis.CLORURO M√ìRFICO:Cloruro m√≥rfico is the analgesic medication administered to manage the patient's pain.HIPERTENSI√ìN:Hipertensi√≥n is a medical condition characterized by high blood pressure, which is documented in the patient's medical history. It is recognized as a significant health issue that can lead to various complications if not managed properly.EPIGASTRALGIA:Epigastralgia refers to pain in the upper central region of the abdomen, which the patient experienced during feeding. This condition is characterized by severe abdominal pain that ultimately led to the patient's hospital admission.NPT:NPT, which stands for Nutritional Parenteral Therapy, is a form of nutritional support initiated for patients during their hospital stay. It is specifically designed to provide essential nutrition to individuals who are unable to eat, ensuring that their dietary needs are met while they are receiving medical care.MURPHY:Murphy's sign is a clinical test used to assess for gallbladder inflammation, which was positive in the patient's examination.LEU 18.000:Leu 18.000 refers to the elevated white blood cell count found in the patient's laboratory tests, indicating possible infection or inflammation.PCR:PCR, or C-reactive protein, is a significant marker of inflammation, as indicated by elevated levels in the patient's laboratory results. It is commonly measured through a blood test that assesses inflammation in the body and is frequently utilized to evaluate various health conditions.TROPNINA ALTA SENSIBILIDAD:Troponina alta sensibilidad is a cardiac marker that was tested to rule out myocardial infarction in the patient.GLUCEMIA 290 MG/DL:Glucemia 290 mg/dL indicates the patient's elevated blood sugar level at the time of admission.CIRUG√çA:El servicio de Cirug√≠a es responsable de la atenci√≥n y tratamiento del paciente, incluyendo la nutrici√≥n parenteral y la colocaci√≥n de sonda para alimentaci√≥n enteral.DIGESTIVO:El servicio de Digestivo es consultado para la colocaci√≥n endosc√≥pica de la sonda para alimentaci√≥n enteral del paciente.RANSON:RANSON refers to a scoring system utilized to evaluate the severity of acute pancreatitis in patients. This system comprises a set of criteria that incorporates both clinical and laboratory findings, allowing healthcare professionals to assess the condition's seriousness effectively. The Ranson criteria are essential for guiding treatment decisions and predicting patient outcomes in cases of acute pancreatitis.APACHE II:APACHE II is a severity scoring system designed to evaluate the prognosis of critically ill patients, including those suffering from acute pancreatitis. This scoring system measures the severity of illness in patients, providing a quantitative assessment that helps in determining the risk of complications. Specifically, a score of 10 or higher on the APACHE II scale indicates an increased risk of adverse outcomes. The tool is widely utilized in clinical settings to guide treatment decisions and improve patient management for those in critical condition.SONDA DE ALIMENTACI√ìN ENTERAL:La sonda de alimentaci√≥n enteral es un dispositivo que se planea colocar para proporcionar nutrici√≥n al paciente de manera directa al yeyuno.OBESIDAD GRADO 1:NUTRICI√ìN PARENTERAL TOTAL (NPT):NPT es un tipo de nutrici√≥n administrada intravenosamente que el paciente ha estado recibiendo para mantener su estado nutricional.SONDA NASOENTERAL:La sonda nasoenteral es un dispositivo m√©dico que se ha colocado en el paciente para proporcionar nutrici√≥n enteral.TC (TOMOGRAF√çA COMPUTARIZADA):La TC es un procedimiento de imagen que se realiz√≥ para evaluar la evoluci√≥n del paciente con pancreatitis aguda.ALBUMINA:Albumin, also known as alb√∫mina, is a protein found in the blood that plays a crucial role in assessing a patient's nutritional status and liver function. It is commonly measured in blood tests, and its levels are monitored as part of liver function tests. Throughout the patient's treatment, albumin levels have varied, indicating fluctuations in their nutritional status and liver health.PREALBUMINA:La prealb√∫mina es otra prote√≠na que se ha medido en el paciente, indicando su estado nutricional a lo largo del tratamiento.VANCOMICINA:La vancomicina es un antibi√≥tico que se ha administrado emp√≠ricamente al paciente durante su tratamiento.HEMOCULTIVO:El hemocultivo es una prueba que se realiz√≥ para detectar infecciones en el paciente, mostrando crecimiento de cocos gram positivos.GLUCEMIAS:GLUCEMIAS se refiere a las mediciones de los niveles de az√∫car en la sangre. Estas mediciones son especialmente importantes en el contexto de pacientes con Alzheimer, ya que se utilizan para ajustar el tratamiento de la enfermedad.DESNUTRICI√ìN:Desnutrici√≥n, or malnutrition, is a condition that can arise in various patient populations due to inadequate nutrient intake and other contributing factors. In patients with cancer, desnutrici√≥n is particularly prevalent, especially among those who also have diabetes mellitus and other comorbidities. The condition can be exacerbated by the increased energy expenditure associated with cancer and the challenges of nutrient absorption. Similarly, patients with cystic fibrosis (FQ) frequently experience malnutrition due to malabsorption of nutrients, which is a common complication of the disease. Additionally, individuals suffering from severe acute pancreatitis may also face desnutrici√≥n as a result of disproportionate catabolism and a systemic inflammatory response. Overall, desnutrici√≥n is a multifaceted issue that can significantly impact the health and recovery of patients across these various medical conditions.HIPERGLUCEMIA:HIPERGLUCEMIA es una condici√≥n caracterizada por niveles elevados de glucosa en sangre. Esta condici√≥n es com√∫n en pacientes diab√©ticos, quienes pueden experimentar hiperglucemia como parte de su enfermedad. Adem√°s, la hiperglucemia tambi√©n se presenta frecuentemente en pacientes con pancreatitis aguda, donde los niveles de glucosa en sangre se encuentran anormalmente altos.REPOSO PANCRE√ÅTICO:El reposo pancre√°tico es una estrategia terap√©utica que se ha utilizado en el tratamiento de la pancreatitis, aunque su eficacia ha sido cuestionada.NUTRICI√ìN ORAL:La nutrici√≥n oral es el m√©todo de alimentaci√≥n que utiliza la ingesta de alimentos por la boca, que puede ser dif√≠cil en casos de pancreatitis aguda.FALLO ORG√ÅNICO:El fallo org√°nico es una complicaci√≥n grave que puede ocurrir en pacientes con pancreatitis aguda, aumentando la mortalidad en casos severos.SEPSIS:La sepsis es una complicaci√≥n potencialmente mortal que puede desarrollarse en pacientes con pancreatitis aguda, relacionada con la inflamaci√≥n sist√©mica.LITIASIS BILIAR:La litiasis biliar es una de las etiolog√≠as m√°s comunes de la pancreatitis aguda, donde los c√°lculos biliares obstruyen el conducto pancre√°tico.CONSUMO EXCESIVO DE ALCOHOL:El consumo excesivo de alcohol es una de las principales causas de pancreatitis aguda, contribuyendo al da√±o del tejido pancre√°tico.CATABOLISMO:El catabolismo es el proceso metab√≥lico que se acelera en pacientes con pancreatitis aguda, llevando a un deterioro del estado nutricional.HIDROELECTROL√çTICOS:Los desequilibrios hidroelectrol√≠ticos son complicaciones comunes en pacientes con pancreatitis aguda, afectando su tratamiento y recuperaci√≥n.NECROSIS DEL PAR√âNQUIMA:La necrosis del par√©nquima pancre√°tico puede ocurrir en casos severos de pancreatitis aguda, complicando el tratamiento y aumentando el riesgo de mortalidad.CITOCINAS:CRITERIOS DE RANSON:Los criterios de Ranson son un conjunto de par√°metros cl√≠nicos utilizados para predecir la gravedad y el pron√≥stico de la pancreatitis aguda.SONDA ENTERAL:La sonda enteral es un dispositivo utilizado para administrar nutrici√≥n directamente al tracto gastrointestinal en pacientes que no pueden alimentarse por v√≠a oral.NECESIDAD DE CIRUG√çA:La necesidad de cirug√≠a puede surgir en pacientes con pancreatitis aguda debido a complicaciones, lo que puede influir en el tipo de soporte nutricional requerido.PANCREATITIS:Pancreatitis is a severe condition characterized by inflammation of the pancreas, leading to various metabolic and nutritional challenges for patients.NE:NE refers to enteral nutrition, a method of delivering nutrition directly to the stomach or intestines, primarily utilized for patients who have difficulties swallowing or cannot eat by mouth. This approach includes nocturnal enteral nutrition, which specifically provides nutrition through a feeding tube during the night, particularly for patients with feeding difficulties (FQ). NE encompasses various forms of nutritional enteral feeding, which is essential for meeting the caloric requirements of patients unable to consume food orally. Additionally, NE involves the use of specialized nutritional formulas, especially in oncology settings, to support the dietary needs of these patients. In summary, NE is a comprehensive term that signifies the various methods and practices of enteral nutrition aimed at ensuring adequate nutritional intake for individuals with specific medical conditions.NP:NP refers to Nutritional Parenteral, a method of providing nutrition intravenously to patients who cannot tolerate enteral feeding. This approach was initially administered to the patient, highlighting its importance in ensuring adequate nutrition when traditional feeding methods are not feasible.INFECTION:Infection is a complication that can arise in patients with pancreatitis, potentially leading to further health issues.SONDA NASOYEYUNAL:Sonda nasoyeyunal is a nasojejunal tube used for enteral feeding in patients who cannot eat normally.SOLUCI√ìN POLIM√âRICA:Soluci√≥n polim√©rica refers to a standard polymeric solution used for enteral nutrition in patients.F√ìRMULAS ESPEC√çFICAS:F√≥rmulas espec√≠ficas are specialized nutritional formulas designed for patients with specific conditions, such as diabetes.DICKERSON RN:Dickerson RN is an author who contributed to research on resting energy expenditure in patients with pancreatitis.VEHE KL:Vehe KL is an author who collaborated on a study regarding resting energy expenditure in patients with pancreatitis.MULLEN JL:Mullen JL is an author involved in research on resting energy expenditure in patients with pancreatitis.FEURER ID:Feurer ID is an author who contributed to the study of resting energy expenditure in patients with pancreatitis.MARULENDRA S:Marulendra S is an author who worked on nutrition support in pancreatitis.KIRBY D:Kirby D is an author who co-authored a paper on nutrition support in pancreatitis.HAVALA T:Havala T is an author who researched nutritional support in acute pancreatitis.SHRONTS E:Shronts E is an author who contributed to the study of nutritional support in acute pancreatitis.CERRA F:Cerra F is an author involved in research on nutritional support in acute pancreatitis.KOV√ÅR J:Kov√°r J is an author who studied the effects of hyperglycemia on lipoprotein lipase activity.FEJFAROV√Å V:Fejfarov√° V is an author who collaborated on research regarding hyperglycemia and lipoprotein lipase activity.PELIK√ÅNOV√Å T:Pelik√°nov√° T is an author who contributed to the study of hyperglycemia's effects on lipoprotein lipase activity.POLEDNE R:Poledne R is an author involved in research on hyperglycemia and lipoprotein lipase activity.HELTON WS:Helton WS is an author who wrote about intravenous nutrition in patients with acute pancreatitis.ROMBEAU JL:Rombeau JL is an editor of a book on clinical nutrition, including parenteral nutrition.KOHN CL:Kohn CL is an author who researched nutritional support for patients with pancreatobiliary disease.BROZENEC S:Brozenec S is an author who contributed to the study of nutritional support for pancreatobiliary disease.FOSTER PF:Foster PF is an author involved in research on nutritional support for pancreatobiliary disease.MCCLAVE SA:McClave SA is an author who conducted a systematic review of nutrition support in acute pancreatitis.CHANGE WK:Change WK is an author who contributed to the systematic review of nutrition support in acute pancreatitis.DHALIWAL R:Dhaliwal R is an author involved in the systematic review of nutrition support in acute pancreatitis.HEYLAND DK:Heyland DK is an author who contributed to the systematic review of nutrition support in acute pancreatitis.MARIK PE:Marik PE is an author who conducted a meta-analysis of parenteral versus enteral nutrition in acute pancreatitis.ZALOGA GPL:Zaloga GPL is an author who collaborated on the meta-analysis of nutrition methods in acute pancreatitis.WINDSOR AC:Windsor AC is an author who researched the effects of enteral feeding in acute pancreatitis.KANWAR S:Kanwar S is an author who contributed to the study comparing enteral and parenteral nutrition in acute pancreatitis.LI AG:Li AG is an author involved in the research comparing enteral and parenteral nutrition in acute pancreatitis.ABOU-ASSI S:Abou-Assi S is an author who studied hypocaloric jejunal feeding in acute pancreatitis.CRAIG K:Craig K is an author who collaborated on the study of hypocaloric jejunal feeding in acute pancreatitis.O'KEEFE SJ:O'Keefe SJ is an author involved in the research on hypocaloric jejunal feeding in acute pancreatitis.POWELL JJ:Powell JJ is an author who conducted a trial on early enteral nutrition in acute pancreatitis.MURCHISON JT:Murchison JT is an author who contributed to the trial of early enteral nutrition in acute pancreatitis.FEARON KC:Fearon KC is an author involved in the trial of early enteral nutrition in acute pancreatitis.LARVIN M:Larvin M is an author who studied the APACHE-II score for assessing acute pancreatitis.MCMAHON MJ:McMahon MJ is an author who contributed to the study of the APACHE-II score in acute pancreatitis.CORFIELD AP:Corfield AP is an author who researched the prediction of severity in acute pancreatitis.COOPER MJ:Cooper MJ is an author who collaborated on the prediction of severity in acute pancreatitis.WILLIAMSON RC:Williamson RC is an author involved in the prediction of severity in acute pancreatitis.KALFARENTZOS F:Kalfarentzos F is an author who studied enteral nutrition in severe acute pancreatitis.KEHAGIAS J:Kehagias J is an author who contributed to the research on enteral nutrition in severe acute pancreatitis.MEAD N:Mead N is an author involved in the study of enteral nutrition in severe acute pancreatitis.LOUIE B:Louie B is an author who assessed health technology regarding enteral or parenteral nutrition for severe pancreatitis.NOSEWORTHY T:Noseworthy T is an author who contributed to the health technology assessment of nutrition methods in severe pancreatitis.HAILEY D:Hailey D is an author involved in the health technology assessment of nutrition methods in severe pancreatitis.CASAS M:Casas M is an author who studied total enteral versus total parenteral nutrition in severe acute pancreatitis.MORA J:Mora J is an author who collaborated on the study of nutrition methods in severe acute pancreatitis.FORT E:Fort E is an author involved in the research comparing total enteral and parenteral nutrition in severe acute pancreatitis.KAUSHIK N:Kaushik N is an author who studied enteral feeding without pancreatic stimulation.PIETRASZEWSKI M:Pietraszewski M is an author who contributed to the research on enteral feeding without pancreatic stimulation.HOLST JJ:Holst JJ is an author involved in the study of enteral feeding without pancreatic stimulation.ECKERWALL GE:Eckerwall GE is an author who researched immediate oral feeding in mild acute pancreatitis.TINGSTEDT BB:Tingstedt BB is an author who contributed to the study of immediate oral feeding in mild acute pancreatitis.BERGENZAUN PE:Bergenzaun PE is an author involved in the research on immediate oral feeding in mild acute pancreatitis.ANDER("ENTITY":EVENTPARENTERAL NUTRITION:Parenteral nutrition is a method of delivering nutrients directly into the bloodstream, bypassing the gastrointestinal tract, often used in patients with severe conditions like acute pancreatitis.HYPERCALORIC JEJUNAL FEEDING:Hypocaloric jejunal feeding is a nutritional strategy that provides fewer calories than the body needs, delivered through the jejunum, often used in patients with acute pancreatitis.RANDOMIZED CONTROLLED TRIAL:A randomized controlled trial is a study design that randomly assigns participants to different interventions to compare the effects of those interventions, often used in clinical research.SYSTEMATIC REVIEW:A systematic review is a research method that summarizes the results of multiple studies on a specific topic to provide a comprehensive understanding of the evidence.META-ANALYSIS:A meta-analysis is a statistical technique that combines the results of multiple studies to identify patterns, differences, or overall effects.PROSPECTIVE STUDY:A prospective study is a research design that follows participants over time to observe outcomes, often used in clinical research to assess the effects of interventions.HEALTH TECHNOLOGY ASSESSMENT:Health technology assessment is a systematic evaluation of the properties and effects of health technology, including its direct and intended effects, as well as its indirect effects.CLINICAL TRIAL:A clinical trial is a research study that tests how well new medical approaches work in people, often involving new drugs, devices, or treatment strategies.INFLAMMATORY RESPONSE:The inflammatory response is the body's reaction to injury or infection, which can be influenced by nutritional support in conditions like acute pancreatitis.APACHE II SCORE:The APACHE II score is a severity-of-disease classification system that is used to assess the prognosis of patients in intensive care, particularly in cases like acute pancreatitis.NUTRITIONAL SUPPORT IN ACUTE PANCREATITIS:Nutritional support in acute pancreatitis refers to the strategies and methods used to provide adequate nutrition to patients suffering from this condition.SEVERE ACUTE PANCREATITIS:Severe acute pancreatitis is a more serious form of acute pancreatitis that can lead to complications and requires intensive medical management.HYPERTRIGLYCERIDEMIA:Hypertriglyceridemia is a condition characterized by elevated levels of triglycerides in the blood, which can be a risk factor for pancreatitis.NUTRITIONAL INTERVENTIONS:Nutritional interventions are strategies implemented to improve the nutritional status of patients, particularly in the context of medical conditions like acute pancreatitis.RANDOMIZED COMPARATIVE STUDY:A randomized comparative study is a type of research that compares two or more interventions in a randomized manner to determine their effectiveness.PROGNOSTIC INDICES:Prognostic indices are tools used to predict the outcome of a disease or condition, often based on clinical and laboratory data.NUTRITIONAL OUTCOMES:Nutritional outcomes refer to the results of nutritional interventions, including improvements in health status, recovery times, and overall well-being.CLINICAL NUTRITION:Clinical nutrition is a field of study that focuses on the role of nutrition in the treatment and management of diseases, including acute pancreatitis.ENTERAL FEEDING:Enteral feeding is a method of delivering nutrition directly to the stomach or intestines, often used in patients who cannot eat normally.TOTAL PARENTERAL NUTRITION:Total parenteral nutrition is a method of providing all nutritional requirements intravenously, bypassing the gastrointestinal tract, used in severe cases of pancreatitis.NUTRITIONAL STRATEGIES:Nutritional strategies are planned approaches to provide adequate nutrition to patients, particularly those with specific medical conditions like acute pancreatitis.NUTRITIONAL GUIDELINES:Nutritional guidelines are recommendations for dietary practices aimed at improving health outcomes, especially in clinical settings.DIETARY INTERVENTIONS:Dietary interventions are changes made to a patient's diet to improve health outcomes, particularly in the context of managing diseases like acute pancreatitis.NUTRITIONAL ASSESSMENT:Nutritional assessment is a comprehensive process aimed at evaluating a patient's nutritional status and needs. This evaluation often employs various tools, such as the Mini Nutritional Assessment (MNA), to gather relevant data. The primary goal of nutritional assessment is to guide dietary and nutritional interventions tailored to the individual patient. It involves a thorough examination of the patient's dietary habits and nutritional requirements, ensuring that any treatment plans are effectively aligned with their specific needs. Overall, nutritional assessment serves as a critical step in understanding and addressing a patient's nutritional health.NUTRITIONAL THERAPY:Nutritional therapy is the use of specific dietary practices to manage health conditions, including acute pancreatitis.NUTRITIONAL EVALUATION:Nutritional evaluation is the process of assessing a patient's dietary intake and nutritional needs to inform treatment plans.NUTRITIONAL RESEARCH:Nutritional research is the scientific study of how diet and nutrition affect health and disease, including conditions like acute pancreatitis.NUTRITIONAL OUTCOMES IN ACUTE PANCREATITIS:Nutritional outcomes in acute pancreatitis refer to the results of nutritional interventions on patients suffering from this condition.NUTRITIONAL STRATEGIES IN ACUTE PANCREATITIS:Nutritional strategies in acute pancreatitis are specific approaches to providing nutrition to patients with this condition.NUTRITIONAL SUPPORT GUIDELINES:Nutritional support guidelines are recommendations for providing nutrition to patients with specific medical conditions, including acute pancreatitis.NUTRITIONAL INTERVENTIONS IN ACUTE PANCREATITIS:Nutritional interventions in acute pancreatitis are specific strategies implemented to improve the nutritional status of affected patients.NUTRITIONAL THERAPY IN ACUTE PANCREATITIS:Nutritional therapy in acute pancreatitis involves dietary modifications and interventions aimed at managing the condition.NUTRITIONAL ASSESSMENT IN ACUTE PANCREATITIS:Nutritional assessment in acute pancreatitis is the evaluation of patients' dietary intake and nutritional needs to guide treatment.NUTRITIONAL OUTCOMES IN SEVERE ACUTE PANCREATITIS:Nutritional outcomes in severe acute pancreatitis refer to the results of nutritional interventions on patients with this more serious form of the condition.NUTRITIONAL STRATEGIES IN SEVERE ACUTE PANCREATITIS:Nutritional strategies in severe acute pancreatitis are specific approaches to providing nutrition to patients with this serious condition.NUTRITIONAL SUPPORT IN SEVERE ACUTE PANCREATITIS:Nutritional support in severe acute pancreatitis refers to the strategies and methods used to provide adequate nutrition to patients suffering from this serious condition.NUTRITIONAL GUIDELINES FOR ACUTE PANCREATITIS:Nutritional guidelines for acute pancreatitis are recommendations for dietary practices aimed at improving health outcomes in affected patients.NUTRITIONAL OUTCOMES IN PANCREATITIS:Nutritional outcomes in pancreatitis referDR. MAR√çA ARGENTE PLA:A medical doctor involved in a clinical case of a diabetic patient with cystic fibrosis.DR. ROSA M¬™ MIRETE L√ìPEZ:A medical doctor involved in a clinical case of a patient with diabetes mellitus and inflammatory bowel disease.GASTROSTOM√çA QUIR√öRGICA:A surgical procedure to create an opening into the stomach for nutritional support, particularly in patients with swallowing difficulties.PACIENTE CON DEMENCIA Y DIABETES MELLITUS TIPO 2:A clinical case involving a patient with dementia and type 2 diabetes mellitus, discussed during the round table event.PACIENTE DIAB√âTICO CON FIBROSIS QU√çSTICA:A clinical case involving a diabetic patient with cystic fibrosis, discussed during the round table event.PACIENTE CON DIABETES MELLITUS Y ENFERMEDAD INFLAMATORIA INTESTINAL:A clinical case involving a patient with diabetes mellitus and inflammatory bowel disease, discussed during the round table event.NUTRICI√ìN ENTERAL EN PACIENTE DIAB√âTICO CON CARCINOMA EPIDERMOIDE DE LARINGE:A clinical case involving enteral nutrition in a diabetic patient with laryngeal squamous cell carcinoma, discussed during the round table event.UNCYD:UNCyD, or Unidad de Nutrici√≥n Cl√≠nica y Diet√©tica, is a clinical nutrition unit dedicated to conducting nutritional assessments and screenings, particularly for patients over the age of 65. This institution operates within a collaborative team environment, offering nutritional support tailored to patients with special needs. Through its comprehensive approach, UNCyD aims to enhance the nutritional well-being of its patients, ensuring they receive the necessary care and resources to address their specific dietary requirements.VAR√ìN DE 77 A√ëOS:A 77-year-old man with severe neurogenic dysphagia who was admitted to the neurology service due to a vertebrobasilar stroke.ENFERMEDAD ALZHEIMER:Alzheimer's disease is a progressive neurological disorder that affects memory and cognitive function, diagnosed in the patient 14 months prior to admission.ICTUS VERTEBRO BASILAR:A vertebrobasilar stroke that the patient experienced, leading to his admission to the hospital.FIBRILACI√ìN AURICULAR:Atrial fibrillation is a heart condition that causes an irregular and often rapid heart rate, which was present in the patient and contributed to his stroke risk.ALTEPLASA:Alteplasa is a medication utilized for fibrinolysis in patients experiencing ischemic stroke, aiding in the dissolution of blood clots. Specifically, alteplase is indicated for thrombolysis in acute ischemic stroke cases. However, in certain situations, such as with specific patients, the administration of alteplase may need to be interrupted shortly after it begins.OLMESART√ÅN:Olmesartan is an oral medication prescribed to patients for managing hypertension, specifically used to treat high blood pressure.DIFICULTAD EN LA INGESTA ORAL:Oral intake difficulty is a condition affecting the patient's ability to consume food normally, leading to the need for nutritional support.HIPOGLICEMIA:TAC CEREBRAL:TAC cerebral is a medical imaging procedure used to diagnose conditions such as ischemic stroke, specifically in this case, the right PICA ischemic stroke.ICTUS ISQU√âMICO:Ictus isqu√©mico refers to a type of stroke caused by a blockage in blood flow to the brain, leading to potential brain damage.HEPARINA:Heparina is a medication used for the prevention of thromboembolic events, particularly in patients at risk of deep vein thrombosis.UNIDAD DE NUTRICI√ìN CL√çNICA Y DIET√âTICA (UNCYD):UNCyD is a clinical nutrition and dietetics unit that provides nutritional assessment and support for patients.UREA:Urea is a waste product measured in the blood to evaluate kidney function and metabolic health.CREATININA:Creatinina is a chemical waste product in the blood that is used to assess kidney function.FG CKD-EPI:FG CKD-EPI is a formula used to estimate the glomerular filtration rate, indicating kidney function.PRO-BNP:Pro-BNP is a biomarker used to diagnose heart failure and assess cardiac function.HEMOGRAMA:Hemograma is a complete blood count test that evaluates overall health and detects a variety of disorders.GASOMETR√çA VENOSA:Gasometr√≠a venosa is a test that measures the levels of gases in the blood, assessing respiratory and metabolic function.COAGULACI√ìN:Coagulaci√≥n tests evaluate the blood's ability to clot, important for assessing bleeding disorders.RADIOGRAF√çA DE T√ìRAX:Radiograf√≠a de t√≥rax is a chest X-ray used to visualize the lungs and heart for any abnormalities.DIETA ABSOLUTA:Dieta absoluta is a medical dietary restriction where no food is consumed, often used in preparation for medical procedures.FLUIDOTERAPIA INTRAVENOSA:Fluidoterapia intravenosa is a medical treatment involving the administration of fluids directly into the bloodstream to maintain hydration and electrolyte balance.INSULINA R√ÅPIDA:Insulina r√°pida is a type of fast-acting insulin utilized to manage blood sugar levels in diabetic patients. It is administered to patients subcutaneously every six hours to effectively control glycemia.CIRCUNFERENCIA BRAQUIAL:The "CIRCUNFERENCIA BRAQUIAL" refers to the measurement of the upper arm, specifically the circumference of the brachial area. This measurement is utilized to assess muscle mass and nutritional status in patients. It plays a significant role during nutritional assessments, providing valuable information regarding an individual's health and nutritional needs.CIRCUNFERENCIA DE PANTORRILLA:Circunferencia de pantorrilla is a measurement of the calf used to assess nutritional status and muscle mass.HIPOINGESTA:Hipoingesta refers to a state of reduced food intake, which can lead to weight loss and nutritional deficiencies.P√âRDIDA DE PESO:"P√âRDIDA DE PESO" refers to a significant health condition observed in patients, characterized by a notable reduction in body weight. In this specific case, the patient experienced a weight loss of 5 kg over the six months leading up to their referral, with a more substantial overall weight loss amounting to 10 kg during their hospital stay. The percentage of weight loss in this context ranges from 13% to 17%, highlighting the severity of the condition. Weight loss is a common occurrence among cancer patients, often linked to the disease itself and the effects of its treatments. This condition is a critical factor in medical assessments, as it can indicate the progression of the illness and the patient's overall health status.RESULTADOS ANAL√çTICOS:Resultados anal√≠ticos refer to the outcomes of various medical tests conducted to evaluate a patient's health.DESCOMPENSACI√ìN HIPERGLUC√âMICA:Descompensaci√≥n hipergluc√©mica refers to a state of elevated blood sugar levels, often requiring medical intervention.GLUCOSA:UNIDAD DE NUTRICI√ìN Y DIET√âTICA:Unidad especializada en la valoraci√≥n y soporte nutricional de pacientes con desnutrici√≥n y otras condiciones relacionadas con la alimentaci√≥nICTUS:ICTUS refers to a stroke, a medical event that can lead to serious complications, including severe neurogenic dysphagia in patients. This condition may complicate the clinical evolution of individuals affected by a stroke, often requiring nutritional support to address the challenges associated with swallowing difficulties.HISTORIA CL√çNICA ELECTR√ìNICA:Sistema donde se registra la historia nutricional y m√©dica del paciente para su seguimientoMINI NUTRITIONAL ASSESSMENT:Herramienta utilizada para evaluar el estado nutricional del paciente, indicando un estado de desnutrici√≥nEAT-10:Test de cribado utilizado para evaluar la disfagia en el pacienteDIFICULTAD PARA INGESTA:Problema que presenta el paciente, que requiere incentivaci√≥n continua para terminar los alimentosCCEE UNCYD:CCEE UNCyD is a clinical center involved in nutritional support and clinical evolution monitoring for patients with specific dietary needs.MECV-V:MECV-V is a clinical evaluation method designed for the assessment of swallowing difficulties, particularly in patients who have experienced a stroke. It serves as a tool for monitoring dysphagia, which refers to difficulties in swallowing. The MECV-V test specifically focuses on volume viscosity, aiding in the diagnosis of swallowing challenges. This method evaluates both the safety and efficiency of swallowing, providing crucial insights into the patient's condition and guiding further treatment.NUTRITION ENTERAL:Nutrition enteral (NE) is a method of delivering nutrition directly to the stomach or intestine through a tube. This approach is specifically utilized for patients who may have difficulty consuming food orally, such as those recovering from surgery. By providing nutrition directly into the gastrointestinal tract, enteral nutrition ensures that patients receive the necessary nutrients for healing and recovery.DESNUTRICI√ìN MODERADA:"DESNUTRICI√ìN MODERADA" refers to a condition diagnosed in the patient, characterized by a significant deficiency in nutritional intake. The diagnosis is based on the GLIM criteria, which indicate a notable loss of weight and essential nutrients. This condition necessitated the implementation of a nutritional treatment plan to address the patient's health and recovery needs. Overall, moderate malnutrition signifies that the patient is experiencing insufficient nutritional intake, which adversely affects their overall well-being.ICTUS VERTEBRO-BASILAR:A vertebrobasilar stroke is a type of stroke that affects the brainstem and can lead to various complications, including dysphagia.DIETA HIPERCAL√ìRICA:A hypercaloric diet is a nutritional plan designed to provide high energy intake, often used for patients with malnutrition.DIETA POLIM√âRICA:A polymeric diet is a type of enteral nutrition that contains whole proteins and is used for patients who can digest food normally.TRATAMIENTO ANTIDEPRESIVO:Antidepressant treatment is a medical intervention aimed at managing symptoms of depression, which can affect a patient's appetite and nutritional intake.VAR√ìN DE 76 A√ëOS:NUTRICI√ìN PARENTERAL TOTAL CENTRAL:Nutritional therapy administered through a central venous access to provide essential nutrients to the patient during hospitalizationNUTRICI√ìN PARENTERAL PERIF√âRICA:Nutritional therapy administered through peripheral veins to maintain the patient's nutritional status before the placement of a gastrostomyGASTROSTOM√çA:Gastrostom√≠a is a surgical procedure that involves creating an opening in the stomach through the abdominal wall, allowing for the placement of a tube that facilitates enteral feeding. This procedure is particularly beneficial for patients who require nutritional support, such as those with low weight or difficulties in oral feeding. The patient in question underwent this procedure at the age of 15 to address their feeding needs and is currently utilizing the gastrostomy tube for nocturnal nutrition. Overall, gastrostom√≠a serves as an essential medical intervention for individuals requiring long-term feeding support.SERVICIO DE CIRUG√çA:Surgical department responsible for evaluating and performing the gastrostomy procedureUNIDAD DE ENDOSCOP√çAS:Department where endoscopic procedures are performed, including attempts to place a gastrostomyCORTICOIDES IV:Intravenous corticosteroids administered to the patient as part of the treatment regimenANTIBIOTICOTERAPIA IV:Intravenous antibiotic therapy provided to the patient to prevent or treat infectionsRADIOLOG√çA INTERVENCIONISTA:Interventional radiology department that attempted to place a gastrostomy tube for the patientFLUIDOTERAPIA IV:ENFERMEDAD DE ALZHEIMER:La enfermedad de Alzheimer es una forma de demencia neurodegenerativa que afecta principalmente a la poblaci√≥n anciana, caracterizada por p√©rdida de memoria y alteraciones en el comportamiento.DRE:La DRE (Desnutrici√≥n Relacionada con la Enfermedad) es una condici√≥n que se identifica y eval√∫a en pacientes con Alzheimer para adaptar su tratamiento nutricional.CEREBRAL HOSPITAL:El hospital de da√±o cerebral (HDC) es un centro especializado donde se contin√∫a el tratamiento y rehabilitaci√≥n de pacientes con da√±o neurol√≥gico.DEMENTIA:Dementia is a progressive neurological condition that affects cognitive function and can lead to complications such as dysphagia.MEC:MEC is a scale used to assess the level of dementia in patients.GDS:GDS is a scale used to measure the severity of dementia.BARTHEL:Barthel is a scale used to measure the degree of dependence in patients.DYSPHAGIA:Dysphagia is a condition characterized by difficulty in swallowing, commonly seen in patients with dementia and can lead to serious complications.FAMILY:Family refers to the relatives of the patient who are involved in decision-making regarding the patient's care and treatment.CAREGIVERS:Caregivers are individuals who provide care and support to patients, often playing a crucial role in the management of patients with dementia.ENDOCINOLOG√çA Y NUTRICI√ìN:Endocrinolog√≠a y nutrici√≥n son servicios m√©dicos que colaboran en el tratamiento de pacientes con problemas nutricionales.CUIDADORES:Cuidadores son personas que asisten a pacientes en su cuidado diario, especialmente en el contexto de enfermedades cr√≥nicas.DIFICULTAD EN EL CONTROL GLUC√âMICO:La dificultad en el control gluc√©mico se refiere a los problemas que enfrentan los pacientes diab√©ticos para mantener niveles adecuados de glucosa en sangre.EVALUACI√ìN NUTRICIONAL:La evaluaci√≥n nutricional es un proceso que determina las necesidades diet√©ticas y de soporte nutricional de un paciente.ACCIDENTE CEREBRO VASCULAR:MAR√çA ARGENTE PLA:Mar√≠a Argente Pla is a specialist in Endocrinology and Nutrition at Hospital Universitario y Polit√©cnico de La Fe in Valencia.HOSPITAL UNIVERSITARIO Y POLIT√âCNICO DE LA FE:Hospital Universitario y Polit√©cnico de La Fe is a reference hospital in Valencia for nutritional assessment and treatment.VALENCIA:Valencia is a city in Spain that is home to several important medical institutions, including the Hospital Universitario Doctor Peset and the Hospital Universitario y Polit√©cnico de La Fe. These hospitals serve as key healthcare facilities in the region, contributing to the city's reputation as a center for medical care and education.F508DEL:F508del is a specific mutation associated with cystic fibrosis that the patient carries.DIABETES RELACIONADA CON LA FIBROSIS QU√çSTICA:Diabetes related to cystic fibrosis (DRFQ) is a significant health condition that affects individuals with cystic fibrosis. It is recognized as the most common non-respiratory complication in cystic fibrosis patients, with a prevalence of approximately 20%. One patient has been managing this diabetes condition since the age of 14, necessitating insulin treatment to help control their blood sugar levels. However, this patient has experienced poor glycemic control, highlighting the challenges associated with managing diabetes in the context of cystic fibrosis.INSULINA LISPRO:Insulina lispro is a rapid-acting insulin utilized by patients as needed for the management of blood sugar levels, particularly during meals. This insulin formulation is designed to help control glucose levels effectively, making it an essential component in the treatment of diabetes.ACETATO DE MEGESTROL:Acetato de megestrol is a medication that is prescribed to increase appetite in patients. It was specifically prescribed to a patient in 2011, and it demonstrated positive effects in enhancing appetite in the subsequent years.TABLA 1:A record that tracks the evolution of the patient's weight, height, BMI, and glycemic control over time.DESNUTRICI√ìN CAL√ìRICA CR√ìNICA GRAVE:A severe chronic caloric malnutrition condition diagnosed in the patient, requiring specialized dietary management.RETRASO DEL CRECIMIENTO:A condition indicating that the patient is not growing at the expected rate, associated with their underlying health issues.PESO IDEAL:The ideal weight for the patient based on their height and body mass index, which is calculated to be 53.5 kg.REQUERIMIENTOS CAL√ìRICOS:The calculated caloric needs for the patient based on their ideal weight and activity level, determined to be 3091 kcal.MEDICINA DIGESTIVA:Medicina Digestiva is a medical department that manages digestive health and treatments.F√ìRMULA PEPT√çDICA HC/HP:F√≥rmula pept√≠dica HC/HP is a specialized nutritional formula designed for patients with specific dietary needs.F√ìRMULA POLIM√âRICA HC/HP:F√≥rmula polim√©rica HC/HP is a nutritional formula used for diabetic patients to manage their dietary intake.CONTROLES DE GLUCEMIA:Controles de glucemia are blood glucose monitoring tests conducted to assess the patient's blood sugar levels.CFTR:CFTR (Cystic Fibrosis Transmembrane Conductance Regulator) is the gene responsible for cystic fibrosis, coding for a chloride channel protein.MUTACI√ìN F508DEL:F508del is the most common mutation associated with cystic fibrosis, affecting the CFTR gene.INSUFICIENCIA PANCRE√ÅTICA EXOCRINA:Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency is a complication of cystic fibrosis that affects digestion and nutrient absorption.INSUFICIENCIA PANCRE√ÅTICA ENDOCRINA:Endocrine pancreatic insufficiency is another complication of cystic fibrosis that can lead to diabetes.CONSULTAS EXTERNAS:The external consultations refer to the medical follow-ups and assessments for the patient with cystic fibrosis.DRFQ:DRFQ refers to a specific type of diabetes diagnosed in patients, necessitating management and treatment strategies that include insulin administration and nutritional education. Additionally, it is associated with malnutrition related to cystic fibrosis, a condition that can worsen the prognosis of patients suffering from this genetic disorder. This highlights the importance of comprehensive care in managing both diabetes and the nutritional challenges faced by individuals with cystic fibrosis.FQ:FQ refers to cystic fibrosis, a genetic disorder that can lead to complications such as malnutrition and requires specific dietary management.SAMUEL:Samuel is a patient diagnosed with DRFQ at the age of 14, who has been undergoing treatment and management for his condition.INSULINA DEGLUDEC:Insulina degludec is a newer long-acting insulin used to improve glycemic control in patients with diabetes.EDUCACI√ìN NUTRICIONAL:Educaci√≥n nutricional refers to the process of educating patients about proper dietary practices to manage their health conditions, particularly diabetes.EJERCICIO AER√ìBICO:Ejercicio aer√≥bico is a form of physical activity recommended for patients to improve their overall health and manage diabetes.MAL CONTROL GLUC√âMICO:Mal control gluc√©mico refers to the poor management of blood glucose levels in patients, which can lead to complications in diabetes.VITAMINAS LIPOSOLUBLES:Fat-soluble vitamins are essential nutrients that need to be monitored in cystic fibrosis patients due to malabsorption issues.√ÅCIDO F√ìLICO:Folic acid is a vitamin that is monitored in cystic fibrosis patients to ensure adequate levels.CALCIO:Calcium is a mineral that is monitored in cystic fibrosis patients to prevent deficiencies.HIERRO:Iron is a vital nutrient that is monitored in cystic fibrosis patients to avoid anemia and deficiencies.F√ìRMULA POLIM√âRICA HIPERCAL√ìRICA HIPERPROTEICA:A type of enteral nutrition formula that is hypercaloric and hyperproteic, specifically designed for diabetic patients.F√ìRMULA PEPT√çDICA:F√ìRMULA PEPT√çDICA is a specialized enteral nutrition formula designed specifically for patients with cystic fibrosis who experience challenges in tolerating other nutritional formulas. This formula contains peptides, which are hydrolyzed proteins, making it suitable for individuals with specific digestive needs. It serves as an important nutritional option for those requiring tailored dietary support due to their condition.F√ìRMULA POLIM√âRICA NORMOCAL√ìRICA NORMOPROTEICA:CYSTIC FIBROSIS:Cystic fibrosis is a genetic disorder that affects the lungs and digestive system, leading to severe respiratory and nutritional challenges.RIORDAN JR:Riordan JR is a researcher known for his work in identifying the cystic fibrosis gene and its implications.SOUTHERN KW:Southern KW is a researcher who conducted a survey of newborn screening for cystic fibrosis in Europe.FARRELL PM:Farrell PM is a researcher who studied the prevalence of cystic fibrosis in the European Union.BALLMANN M:Ballmann M is a researcher involved in clinical trials related to diabetes management in cystic fibrosis patients.HEPATOPAT√çA RELACIONADA CON LA FQ:Hepatopathy related to cystic fibrosis refers to liver disease that can occur as a complication of cystic fibrosis.INSUFICIENCIA PANCRE√ÅTICA:Pancreatic insufficiency is a condition where the pancreas does not produce enough enzymes for digestion, commonly associated with cystic fibrosis.ENFERMEDAD PULMONAR:Pulmonary disease in cystic fibrosis involves chronic inflammation and infection in the lungs, a major aspect of the disease.ANOREXIA:Anorexia in the context of cystic fibrosis refers to a loss of appetite that can occur due to chronic illness and treatment side effects.S√çNDROME DE OBSTRUCCI√ìN INTESTINAL DISTAL:Distal intestinal obstruction syndrome is a complication of cystic fibrosis that leads to bowel obstruction.SOBRECRECIMIENTO BACTERIANO:Bacterial overgrowth is a condition that can occur in cystic fibrosis patients due to altered gut flora and digestive issues.ALTERACI√ìN DE LA MOTILIDAD INTESTINAL:Altered intestinal motility is a common issue in cystic fibrosis, affecting digestion and nutrient absorption.F√ìRMULA POLIM√âRICA HC:A specific type of nutritional formula designed for diabetic patients, particularly those with cystic fibrosis.F√ìRMULA EST√ÅNDAR:Standard formula refers to a nutritional formula used for general dietary needs, which may differ from specialized formulas for specific conditions.F√ìRMULA DIABETES:Diabetes formula is a specialized nutritional formula designed to manage blood sugar levels in diabetic patients.HOSPITAL UNIVERSITARIO DE SAN JUAN:The Hospital Universitario de San Juan is a medical facility located in Alicante, Spain, where the patient received treatment for diabetes and abdominal issues.ROSA M¬™ MIRETE L√ìPEZ:Dr. Rosa M¬™ Mirete L√≥pez is a specialist in Endocrinology and Nutrition who treated the patient.SEPTIEMBRE 2017:The month and year when the patient was admitted to the hospital for treatment of an abdominal abscess.ABSCESE ABDOMINAL:An abdominal abscess is a collection of pus that can occur in the abdomen, which was the reason for the patient's urgent admission.S√çNDROME DE INTESTINO CORTO:Short bowel syndrome is a condition that can occur after surgical removal of a significant portion of the intestine, which was relevant to the patient's treatment.FLUIDOTERAPIA ENDOVENOSA:Intravenous fluid therapy is a treatment method used to provide fluids directly into the bloodstream, which was used for the patient during her recovery.TUMORACI√ìN ABDOMINAL GIGANTE:The patient presented with a giant abdominal tumor, which was a significant factor in her urgent admission.LAPAROTOM√çA EXPLORADORA:Exploratory laparotomy is a surgical procedure conducted to investigate the underlying causes of a patient's abdominal problems. On March 25, 1998, an exploratory laparotomy was performed, which revealed the presence of neoplasia in the pancreas along with multiple nodules in the liver.DEHISCENCIA DE ANASTOMOSIS:Anastomotic dehiscence is a complication that occurred post-surgery, indicating a failure of the surgical connection.SEPSIS GRAVE:Sepsis Grave, or severe sepsis, is a life-threatening condition that occurs when the body's response to an infection leads to significant injury to its own tissues and organs. This serious medical condition can develop as a result of various infections, including peritonitis, which is an inflammation of the peritoneum often caused by bacterial infection. In such cases, the body's overwhelming response to the infection can result in severe complications, making timely medical intervention critical for patient survival.ILEOSTOM√çA TERMINAL:A terminal ileostomy is a surgical procedure that involves creating an opening in the abdominal wall to allow waste to exit the body after the removal of part of the intestine. This procedure is typically performed on patients who experience complications that necessitate such an intervention.ANASTOMOSIS YEYUNOC√ìLICA:Yeyuno-colic anastomosis is a surgical procedure performed to connect the jejunum to the colon after resection of the ileum.YEYUNOSTOM√çA DE DESCARGA:The "YEYUNOSTOM√çA DE DESCARGA" refers to a jejunostomy procedure specifically designed for decompression. This surgical intervention involves creating an opening in the jejunum to alleviate intestinal obstruction or to manage excessive output from an ileostomy. While the procedure is essential for diverting intestinal contents due to the patient's medical condition, it also carries the risk of leading to nutritional deficiencies. Therefore, careful management of nutrition is crucial following the surgery to ensure the patient's health and well-being.SERVICIO DE CIRUG√çA GENERAL:The General Surgery Service is responsible for the surgical treatment of patients, including those with complex abdominal conditions.E. DE CROHN:E. de Crohn is an inflammatory bowel disease that causes inflammation of the digestive tract, leading to various complications.PERITONITIS FECALOIDEA:Fecaloid peritonitis is an inflammation of the peritoneum caused by the leakage of intestinal contents into the abdominal cavity, often requiring surgical intervention.DRENADO QUIR√öRGICO:Surgical drainage is a procedure performed to remove pus or fluid from an abscess, which was done for the patient‚Äôs abscess in the right flank.LAPARATOM√çA EXPLORADORA:Exploratory laparotomy is a surgical procedure to open the abdomen and examine the organs, which was performed on the patient to assess her condition.ANASTOMOSIS ILEOTRANSVERSA:Ileotransverse anastomosis is a surgical connection made between the ileum and the transverse colon, which was part of the patient‚Äôs surgical treatment.D√âBITO FECALOIDEO:Fecaloid output refers to the discharge of fecal matter from a surgical site, which the patient experienced post-surgery.F√çSTULA INTESTINAL:An intestinal fistula is an abnormal connection between the intestine and another organ or the skin, which the patient was suspected to have developed.DRENADO Y DESBRIDAMIENTO:Drainage and debridement are surgical procedures to remove infected tissue and fluid, which were performed on the patient during her treatment.DULAGLUTIDE:Dulaglutide es un medicamento utilizado para el tratamiento de la diabetes en la paciente.SIC:SIC, or s√≠ndrome de intestino corto, is a medical condition characterized by the preservation of less than 115 cm of small intestine. This condition can result in insufficient intestinal length, which may lead to the necessity for nutritional support to meet the patient's dietary needs.GRUPO ANDALUZ DE NUTRICI√ìN E INVESTIGACI√ìN:Este grupo ha publicado recomendaciones sobre el manejo de la glucemia en pacientes que inician nutrici√≥n artificial.COLESTIRAMINA:Colestiramina es un medicamento que la paciente est√° tomando para ayudar a controlar el colesterol.CODE√çNA:Code√≠na es un medicamento que la paciente est√° tomando para el manejo del dolor.KREON:Kreon es un medicamento que la paciente est√° tomando para ayudar en la digesti√≥n de los alimentos.LOPERAMIDA:ASPEN:ASPEN, the American Society for Parenteral and Enteral Nutrition, is an organization dedicated to providing comprehensive guidelines on the screening and management of malnutrition in patients, with a particular focus on those in the oncological context. The organization plays a crucial role in establishing standards for nutritional support, especially for patients who require parenteral nutrition. Through its efforts, ASPEN aims to enhance the quality of care for individuals facing nutritional challenges due to medical conditions.TEDUGLUTIDE:Teduglutide is a medication that promotes intestinal adaptation and reduces the need for parenteral nutrition in patients with intestinal failure.GU√çAS EUROPEAS:The European guidelines offer protocols for nutritional management in patients with intestinal disorders.HIDROELECTROL√çTICAS:Hydroelectrolytic alterations refer to imbalances in electrolytes that can occur in patients with intestinal issues.DIARREA SEVERA:Severe diarrhea is a condition that can lead to significant nutritional deficiencies and may require parenteral nutrition.NUTRICI√ìN PARENTERAL DOMICILIARIA:Home parenteral nutrition (NPD) is a method of providing nutritional support to patients at home.F√ìRMULAS POLIM√âRICAS:Polymeric formulas are nutritional products that are generally well tolerated and used in enteral nutrition.F√ìRMULAS SEMIELEMENTALES:Semi-elemental formulas are specialized nutritional products used for patients with extensive disease affecting absorption.F√ìRMULAS ELEMENTALES:Elemental formulas are highly processed nutritional products that should be avoided due to their high osmolarity and cost.GLUTAMINA:Glutamine is an amino acid that may be used in nutritional support but is not recommended due to inconclusive evidence.GH:Growth hormone (GH) is sometimes considered for nutritional support but lacks sufficient evidence for its effectiveness.INSULINOTERAPIA:Insulin therapy, known as "insulinoterapia," is a crucial treatment for managing blood glucose levels, particularly in patients who are receiving nutritional support. This therapy is especially important for individuals with diabetes, as it helps regulate their blood sugar levels. Additionally, insulin therapy may be necessary for patients with pancreatic cancer, highlighting its significance in managing diabetes in the context of serious health conditions.ESTUDIO STEPS:The STEPS study investigated the effects of teduglutide on patients with short bowel syndrome and their nutritional needs.ADAPTACI√ìN INTESTINAL:Intestinal adaptation refers to the process by which the intestine adjusts to changes in nutrition and absorption capabilities.GU√çAS AMERICANAS:CHRISTIAN SALOM VENDRELL:Dr. Christian Salom Vendrell is a specialist in endocrinology and nutrition at Hospital Universitario Doctor Peset.CARCINOMA EPIDERMOIDE DE LARINGE:Carcinoma epidermoide de laringe is a type of cancer that affects the larynx. It is commonly observed in patients with neoplasms of the head and neck. The patient has been diagnosed with this condition and is currently undergoing treatment for it.GASTROSTOM√çA RADIOL√ìGICA PERCUT√ÅNEA:Gastrostom√≠a radiol√≥gica percut√°nea is a procedure performed on the patient to assist with feeding due to his condition.LARINGUECTOM√çA TOTAL:Laringuectom√≠a total es una cirug√≠a que implica la extirpaci√≥n completa de la laringe. Este procedimiento se realiza com√∫nmente en pacientes con c√°ncer de laringe. El paciente est√° programado para someterse a esta intervenci√≥n quir√∫rgica, que es necesaria para tratar la enfermedad.VACIAMIENTO FUNCIONAL DERECHO:Vaciamiento funcional derecho is a surgical procedure related to the patient's cancer treatment.RADIOTERAPIA:Radioterapia, or radiotherapy, is a treatment method that utilizes radiation to eliminate cancerous cells and is frequently employed in the treatment of neoplasms. It is considered a potential treatment option for patients following surgery, and it plays a significant role in the overall cancer treatment plan for patients.HOSPITAL UNIVERSITARIO DOCTOR PESSET:GASTROSTOM√çA RADIOLOGICA PERCUT√ÅNEA:Gastrostom√≠a radiol√≥gica percut√°nea es un procedimiento m√©dico que permite la alimentaci√≥n a trav√©s de un tubo insertado en el est√≥mago, utilizado en pacientes con dificultades para tragar.AFAGIA:Afagia es la incapacidad para tragar, que puede ser consecuencia de intervenciones quir√∫rgicas o condiciones m√©dicas.MUCOSITIS:Mucositis is the inflammation of the mucous membranes, often occurring as a side effect of radiotherapy. It is a common complication in patients undergoing radiotherapy and chemotherapy, significantly impacting their ability to eat.SUPLEMENTOS NUTRICIONALES ORALES:Suplementos nutricionales orales son productos dise√±ados para complementar la dieta y proporcionar nutrientes adicionales, utilizados en pacientes con deficiencias nutricionales.UNIDAD DE NUTRICI√ìN:La Unidad de Nutrici√≥n es un departamento m√©dico que se encarga de evaluar y ajustar la nutrici√≥n de los pacientes, especialmente aquellos con condiciones complejas como c√°ncer.CCEE:CCEE, or Consultas Externas Especializadas, refers to specialized medical services that provide care to patients who do not require continuous hospitalization. These services focus on offering specialized follow-up and treatment, ensuring that patients receive the necessary medical attention without the need for an inpatient stay.RIESGO NUTRICIONAL:Riesgo nutricional se refiere a la posibilidad de que un paciente no reciba suficientes nutrientes, especialmente en el contexto de enfermedades como el c√°ncer.INTERVENCI√ìN NUTRICIONAL:Intervenci√≥n nutricional es el proceso de evaluar y modificar la dieta de un paciente para mejorar su estado nutricional, especialmente en pacientes oncol√≥gicos.QUIMIOTERAPIA:QUIMIOTERAPIA, or chemotherapy, is a treatment method primarily used for cancer that involves the administration of drugs designed to kill cancer cells. This approach is often employed in the management of various types of cancer, including pancreatic cancer. While chemotherapy can be an effective option, particularly after surgical interventions, it is associated with several complications and side effects. Patients undergoing chemotherapy may experience issues such as hyperglycemia and cachexia, which can significantly impact their nutritional status. Overall, chemotherapy is a critical component in cancer treatment, but it requires careful management due to its potential adverse effects on the patient's health and nutrition.HOMBRE:UN:The UN, in this context, refers to a medical unit that plays a crucial role in the nutritional assessment and treatment of patients, especially in complex cases. Additionally, the UN encompasses the nursing unit responsible for the overall care of the patient, which includes daily wound care and dietary recommendations. This dual focus highlights the UN's commitment to providing comprehensive medical support and specialized nursing care to enhance patient outcomes.ORL:ORL refers to the Otorrinolaringolog√≠a department, also known as the field of Otolaryngology, which specializes in the diagnosis and treatment of conditions related to the ear, nose, and throat. In this context, ORL is where the patient was referred for evaluation and treatment of laryngeal carcinoma. The department is actively involved in the patient's treatment and follow-up care, ensuring comprehensive management of their condition.GRP:GRP refers to a prophylactic gastrostomy placement that was requested for the patient, serving as a medical intervention to provide nutritional support. This type of feeding tube is utilized for patients who are unable to take food orally, indicating its importance in ensuring adequate nutrition. The gastrostomy route through which the patient received this nutritional support is critical for their recovery post-surgery, highlighting the essential role of GRP in the patient's overall treatment plan.MALNUTRITION SCREENING TOOL:The Malnutrition Screening Tool (MST) is a standardized instrument designed to evaluate the risk of malnutrition in patients. It is utilized in clinical settings to identify individuals who may be at risk, allowing for timely intervention and management of their nutritional needs. The MST was specifically applied to this patient to assess their malnutrition risk.VAR√ìN DE 66 A√ëOS:The patient is a 66-year-old male who has undergone surgery for laryngeal carcinoma and is receiving nutritional support.TOMOGRAF√çA COMPUTERIZADA:Tomograf√≠a computerizada (CT) is a diagnostic imaging procedure that revealed the extent of the patient's cancer.AC√öFENOS:Ac√∫fenos refers to the ringing or buzzing in the ears that the patient experienced, prompting further medical evaluation.TOMOGRAF√çA COMPUTERIZADA DE CUELLO:Tomograf√≠a computerizada de cuello is a diagnostic imaging procedure that revealed the presence of neoplasia and adenopathies in the patient.NEOPLASIA:Neoplasia refers to the abnormal growth of tissue, which was identified in the patient's larynx during a CT scan. This condition is characterized by the presence of a tumor, which is pertinent to the patient's medical situation and has implications for their nutritional needs.COMIT√â DE TUMORES:The Comit√© de Tumores is a medical committee dedicated to evaluating patients' cases to determine appropriate treatment options for cancer. This committee plays a crucial role in assessing cancer diagnoses and formulating treatment plans, ensuring that patients receive tailored and effective care based on their specific medical needs.HIPOGLUCEMIA:Hipo glucemia, also known as hypoglycemia, refers to a condition characterized by abnormally low blood sugar levels. This condition poses a risk to patients, particularly those managing diabetes, especially if they are undergoing insulin therapy. It is crucial for individuals with diabetes to monitor their blood sugar levels closely to prevent the occurrence of hypoglycemia.CENTRO DE ESPECIALIDADES:DM:DM refers to diabetes mellitus, a condition that necessitates specific nutritional formulas and insulin management for effective treatment. Additionally, diabetes mellitus complicates the management of patients with rumination syndrome, highlighting the need for careful consideration of both conditions in clinical practice.RT:RT refers to radiotherapy, a treatment that can lead to complications such as mucositis in patients. Additionally, it is noted that the patient underwent RT, which affected their appetite and weight.CDEE:CCEE refers to the outpatient clinic where the patient was monitored after hospital discharge.GLARGINA:Glargina is a type of insulin used for basal insulin therapy in the patient's treatment plan.(N␀␀␀␀␀␀•N␀␀␀␀␀␀O␀␀␀␀␀␀∂O␀␀␀␀␀␀WP␀␀␀␀␀␀†R␀␀␀␀␀␀JS␀␀␀␀␀␀ÔS␀␀␀␀␀␀*V␀␀␀␀␀␀_X␀␀␀␀␀␀ÚX␀␀␀␀␀␀æZ␀␀␀␀␀␀,[␀␀␀␀␀␀ü[␀␀␀␀␀␀\␀␀␀␀␀␀r\␀␀␀␀␀␀]␀␀␀␀␀␀è]␀␀␀␀␀␀˘^␀␀␀␀␀␀␀_␀␀␀␀␀␀Ì_␀␀␀␀␀␀ta␀␀␀␀␀␀øb␀␀␀␀␀␀(e␀␀␀␀␀␀¥e␀␀␀␀␀␀Xf␀␀␀␀␀␀Ôf␀␀␀␀␀␀©g␀␀␀␀␀␀}h␀␀␀␀␀␀íh␀␀␀␀␀␀3i␀␀␀␀␀␀⁄i␀␀␀␀␀␀zj␀␀␀␀␀␀k␀␀␀␀␀␀∆k␀␀␀␀␀␀Äl␀␀␀␀␀␀ïl␀␀␀␀␀␀Ém␀␀␀␀␀␀;n␀␀␀␀␀␀Ân␀␀␀␀␀␀xp␀␀␀␀␀␀5q␀␀␀␀␀␀Õr␀␀␀␀␀␀Ps␀␀␀␀␀␀–s␀␀␀␀␀␀At␀␀␀␀␀␀¿t␀␀␀␀␀␀]u␀␀␀␀␀␀€u␀␀␀␀␀␀Sv␀␀␀␀␀␀ªv␀␀␀␀␀␀-w␀␀␀␀␀␀ìw␀␀␀␀␀␀x␀␀␀␀␀␀|x␀␀␀␀␀␀˝x␀␀␀␀␀␀ty␀␀␀␀␀␀ﬂy␀␀␀␀␀␀@z␀␀␀␀␀␀¢z␀␀␀␀␀␀{␀␀␀␀␀␀Ñ{␀␀␀␀␀␀Ï{␀␀␀␀␀␀U|␀␀␀␀␀␀´|␀␀␀␀␀␀!}␀␀␀␀␀␀ö}␀␀␀␀␀␀ˇ}␀␀␀␀␀␀f~␀␀␀␀␀␀«~␀␀␀␀␀␀'␀␀␀␀␀␀ñ␀␀␀␀␀␀ˆ␀␀␀␀␀␀fÄ␀␀␀␀␀␀‹Ä␀␀␀␀␀␀HÅ